WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.449
Every remote call can fail before sending, during processing, or after the

00:00:04.449 --> 00:00:07.044
server commits but before the reply arrives.

00:00:07.194 --> 00:00:11.599
Configure connection and response timeouts and fit them within a total request

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:11.966
deadline.

00:00:12.116 --> 00:00:16.143
A retry is safe only when the operation’s semantics and identity support

00:00:16.143 --> 00:00:16.478
it.

00:00:16.628 --> 00:00:20.952
Use bounded attempts with backoff and jitter for genuinely retryable failures.

00:00:21.102 --> 00:00:25.911
Circuit breakers reduce calls to a failing dependency, while bulkheads limit how

00:00:25.911 --> 00:00:28.315
much capacity one dependency can occupy.

00:00:28.465 --> 00:00:30.446
Neither creates business correctness.

00:00:30.596 --> 00:00:34.772
Avoid holding database locks or transactions across slow network calls.

00:00:34.922 --> 00:00:39.492
Record unknown outcomes explicitly instead of guessing that a timeout means rejection.

00:00:39.642 --> 00:00:44.470
InventoryClient uses a one-second connection timeout and a two-second read timeout.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.670
A 409 or 404 becomes a known rejection; transport failures and server

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:50.932
errors remain UNKNOWN.

00:00:51.082 --> 00:00:55.762
OrderService leaves the order PENDING and can reconcile by repeating the same

00:00:55.762 --> 00:00:56.542
reservation identity.

00:00:56.692 --> 00:01:00.556
A test exercises an actual delayed HTTP response.

00:01:00.706 --> 00:01:04.916
Run the inventory mock in success, outage, and delayed scenarios.

00:01:05.066 --> 00:01:07.030
Start orders against that endpoint.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.475
Verify that a timeout produces PENDING and that retrying after recovery confirms

00:01:11.475 --> 00:01:12.549
the same order.

00:01:12.699 --> 00:01:16.343
Preserve the idempotency key and order ID across attempts.

00:01:16.493 --> 00:01:19.504
Do not translate a timeout into insufficient stock.

00:01:19.654 --> 00:01:24.052
Measure the elapsed time and verify that the HTTP client’s timeout is

00:01:24.052 --> 00:01:24.418
active.

00:01:24.568 --> 00:01:29.584
The mock demonstrates caller behavior; the real inventory smoke test verifies the

00:01:29.584 --> 00:01:30.420
normal integration.

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:31.242
Question.

00:01:31.392 --> 00:01:32.823
What is a retry storm?

00:01:32.973 --> 00:01:33.549
Answer.

00:01:33.699 --> 00:01:39.276
Many callers repeatedly retrying a failing dependency, multiplying load and delaying recovery.

00:01:39.426 --> 00:01:40.098
Question.

00:01:40.248 --> 00:01:42.700
Why keep the order pending after a timeout?

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:43.426
Answer.

00:01:43.576 --> 00:01:48.450
Inventory may have reserved stock even though the response was lost; reconciliation

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:49.262
is required.
